//
// @PLN89 differential-oracle corpus — AXIS: the call-stack cap.
//
// Two backends, two guards: `State::fn_call` reads `call_stack.len()`, the generated
// binary reads its shadow stack in `cr_call_push`.  One cap enforced in two places is
// the shape that drifts, and it had (loft#1058): the guards counted different things,
// so `rec(9999)` printed an answer on `--interpret` and halted on `--native`, and when
// they did agree that a program had overflowed they said so in two different spellings
// — no `-->` block or caret on native, its own frame format, and the depth count only
// on that side.
//
// One program checks both halves, and pins the boundary from both directions:
//   * 9998 must ANSWER — main + 9999 frames is exactly MAX_CALL_DEPTH (10 000), so a
//     budget that shrank by one on either backend fails here rather than passing
//     quietly one call short of the cap;
//   * 9999 must HALT — one frame past, so a budget that grew by one fails too.
// The stdout line lands before the halt, so the value channel is compared as well as
// the exit code and the diagnostic on stderr.
//
// @ORACLE_NO_WASM: the wasm engine's stack is exhausted between 4 000 and 8 000 frames,
// so the 10 000-frame cap this program is about cannot be reached there at all — the
// module aborts (exit 134, no output) before either half runs.  That gap is loft#1059,
// filed with the measurement; it is not this program's axis and hiding it here would be
// the misuse `wasm_opt_out` refuses.

fn rec1058(n: integer) -> integer {
  if n <= 0 { return 0; }
  return rec1058(n - 1) + 1;
}

fn main() {
  print("at_limit={rec1058(9998)}\n");
  print("past_limit={rec1058(9999)}\n");
}
